			<div class="content" id="post-content-2450623">
									

<p>1) I find needless exposition significantly more immersion breaking than having to google a word.</p>
<p>2) If it's intended for a layman audience, use layman terms. From an in-character perspective it's completely pointless to have both, since you're just saying the same thing twice, with nothing gained. Going back to the medical note comparison, if I'm writing something to give to a non-medical person (like a soldier's commander), I'll just use the layman term instead of trying to teach a grunt a new SAT word. From an out-of-character perspective it's&#8230; equally pointless. The reading experience is not enhanced by having both, or harmed by just having the common term. It's basic prose writing- don't use two words when one will do.</p>

