<p>2. Is it intended for a layman audience? I suppose. I don't believe that should restrict people from using terms that aren't common knowledge. After all, why are there footnotes otherwise? What's the point of having the option to use it if it's frowned upon to include? I'll stick to my guns here as well and state that as long as using footnotes is an available tool we can use, that I support the use of them. It gives the writer more freedom to incorporate terms that I believe add just a bit to the article. Once again, I guess that's just an opinion. Just to be clear, I personally like when I see footnotes</p>

